WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.940
Hey there, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 42 from May 18th, 2026.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:10.880
Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.180
Let's get into it.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:17.720
So imagine you have a super patient friend who knows exactly how to organize information

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.180
and turns your casual descriptions into something that actually works.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.940
That friend is basically what one writer experienced when they teamed up with the AI chatbot

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.060
Claude to build a simple database from scratch.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.980
They created a tool for tracking everyday complaints and gripes,

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.840
like those small annoyances that pile up over time.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.680
Think of a database like a digital notebook that keeps everything sorted and easy to search,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.740
except this one builds itself based on what you describe.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.640
The whole process felt more like brainstorming out loud with a helpful buddy than sitting through

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:57.180
any kind of programming lesson. This matters because it shows how AI is lowering the barrier

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.700
so students or hobbyists can experiment with building apps without years of study.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.960
For example, a teen could sketch out a homework tracker or a fan club organizer

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.720
just by describing what they want in plain words.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.720
It changes daily life by letting creative ideas move from,

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.840
I wish I could make that, to hear it is in one afternoon.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.380
You might start noticing how your own random thoughts could become real projects

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.200
with the right help.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.560
Right now, you can try the same approach yourself by heading to the Claude website,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.620
starting a new chat and describing a simple tool you would like to build.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:35.800
Maybe a list for your favorite songs or a way to track game scores,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.680
and ask it step-by-step how to make it work.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.480
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.780
where we look under the hood at how this kind of help actually happens.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.660
You know how when you are texting a friend,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.860
your phone guesses the next word before you even finish typing?

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.360
Now imagine that same auto-complete got millions of times smarter

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.300
because it studied billions of conversations, stories, and code examples all at once.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.940
Instead of just suggesting one word, it can suggest whole paragraphs,

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.200
fixed mistakes, or even write the code that makes a simple app run.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.040
Step-by-step it breaks your request into tiny pieces,

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.060
predicts what should come next based on patterns it has seen before,

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.400
and checks its own work as it goes.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:25.020
And that is basically what a large language model is doing when it helps someone build a database

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.420
without writing code themselves.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:31.860
So next time someone says AI coding, you can tell them it is basically the world's

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.120
most powerful auto-complete that learned from everything humans have ever written down.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:41.200
Not so scary, right? Now let us look at some cool stuff you can try right away.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.620
Starting with a new way chat GPT is helping with personal money questions.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.720
This feature lets the chatbot connect directly to your bank accounts so it can look at your

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.720
spending and give tailored suggestions like saving tips or budget ideas.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.000
It turns the tool into a helpful money buddy instead of just answering general questions,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.360
which is great for learning good habits early.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:07.040
Head to the chat GPT website, look for the finance tools section,

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.640
and connect a test account if available.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.360
Then try asking it to spot patterns in sample spending data you make up,

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.540
like how to save for a new game console.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:22.840
Next up is something fun for school projects or social media posts using GPT image 2

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.400
to create wild food infographics.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.080
People are turning simple food facts into colorful pictures that explain nutrition or recipes

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.500
at a glance. It feels like having an artist who instantly draws exactly what you describe,

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:40.000
which makes information much easier to share and remember.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:46.480
Open chat GPT, switch to image mode if available, and type something like,

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:50.900
make an infographic showing why apples are healthy with fun cartoon drawings.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:56.180
Experiment with different styles until you get one you like and see how it brings facts

00:03:56.180 --> 00:04:01.400
to life in seconds. Before we wrap up, here are a couple of quick bits worth knowing.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:08.360
Users notice newer versions of chat GPT and Claude now pause more often to give ethical warnings

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.960
even on ordinary creative requests. It shows companies trying hard to keep AI safe,

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:16.560
though it can sometimes feel overly cautious.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.180
Some people are also running into blocks when they try to re-style personal pictures into

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:26.440
anime style, which highlights how AI safety roles keep evolving.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:31.820
It sparks conversations about where helpful creativity ends and overprotection begins.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:38.340
That is it for today. Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:44.700
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That is literally how learning works.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.240
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we will see you tomorrow.

